# Banned-language exemption ledger — hypatia / governance-reusable.yml
#
# Format: <rule_module>/<rule_type>:<path>
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# The governance "Language / package anti-pattern policy" gate is correct:
# these files really are in languages estate policy bans. The gate's own
# failure message names this file as the sanctioned way to declare an
# intentional exception.
#
# These exemptions HOLD THE LINE WHILE MIGRATION IS IN PROGRESS. Each entry
# is removed as the matching file is ported or deleted. This follows the
# precedent set in hyperpolymath/echidna.
#
# EVERY PATH IS LISTED INDIVIDUALLY — deliberately. A `src/**` wildcard would
# silently absorb NEW banned files added later, turning a migration ledger into
# a permanent blind spot. Listing each path means a newly added file still
# fails the gate: this ledger can only shrink as work is done, never quietly
# grow.
#
# CURRENT STATE: EMPTY — no exemptions claimed.
#
# This ledger previously carried exactly one entry:
#
#     cicd_rules/banned_language_file:examples/SafeDOMExample.res
#
# That entry is now dead. `examples/` contains only `quickstart.jl`; there is no
# `SafeDOMExample.res` anywhere in this checkout, and no file with a `.res`,
# `.rs` or `.gleam` extension exists in the repository at all. The exemption was
# inherited from the estate-wide inventory below and outlived the file it
# exempted.
#
# A compliance ledger that claims an exemption for a file that does not exist is
# not harmless bookkeeping: it states that this repository contains banned
# material and has been granted permission to keep it. Leaving it in place would
# also mask a real regression — a reader auditing the language-policy gate would
# conclude the ReScript migration here was still in progress when it is complete.
#
# Per the rule above, this ledger can only shrink as work is done. It has now
# shrunk to zero, which is its intended end state. The file is retained (rather
# than deleted) because it is the sanctioned location the governance gate names
# in its failure message: if a future change genuinely needs an exemption, it is
# declared here, individually, by path, with a reason.
